export default "## Stardust's Privacy Positioning\n\nStardust launched after Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization raised public concern about period tracker privacy. The app markets itself as privacy-conscious, and it has attracted users looking for a Flo alternative on those grounds.\n\nThe marketing is not dishonest. Stardust states it does not sell data, and there is no evidence otherwise. But there is a distinction worth understanding before paying $7.99/mo for it.\n\n## Policy Privacy vs Architectural Privacy\n\nStardust's privacy is policy-based. The company commits to not misusing your data. This is the same commitment every period tracker makes, including Flo before the FTC found otherwise in 2021.\n\nPolicy-based privacy depends on three things:\n- The company maintaining its current commitment\n- No breach exposing server-side data\n- No court order compelling data production\n\nStardust requires an account and stores data on its servers. Policy language cannot change these facts. A subpoena to Stardust's servers would produce your data regardless of what the privacy policy says.\n\nArchitectural privacy works differently. If data is stored on your device and never sent to a server, there is no readable central record to subpoena.\n\n## Policy Privacy vs Paying for Architecture\n\nStardust Premium costs $69.99/yr for policy-based privacy on Stardust's servers. Floriva is a paid app with no free tier that keeps data on-device instead.\n\nIf the astrology features are the draw, Stardust is the only option. If the draw is privacy, the architecture matters more than the brand positioning.";
